## HTTP Analysis ##


## HTTP Request Packet Diagram ##

a) HTTP request/response explanation
Packet(s)	Explanation
4–5	The user opened http://192.168.56.2/. It made a GET to the OpenWRT web server at /. The server responded with the following HTTP response: HTTP/1.1 200 OK, Home page.
9–10	The user has clicked the link to the student page. The browser sent GET /12345678.html. The server has returned the new HTML page, in response to the request.
11–12	The external style sheet has been referenced in the HTML file. A browser automatically requested get / mystyle.css. The server returned the stylesheet, with the HTTP/1.1 200 OK status code.

b) Five address values for the first HTTP request/response
For the first HTTP request, packet 4:
Layer	Address Value
Source MAC	08:00:27:aa:bb:cc
Destination MAC	08:00:27:dd:ee:ff
Source IP	192.168.56.1
Destination IP	192.168.56.2
TCP Ports / Application	Source port 51514, destination port 80 HTTP

c) Did clicking the date/time button send a request?
No. No further HTTP request was made to the server by clicking on the Show date and time button. The date/time function is implemented in the browser, so the browser does not need to communicate with the OpenWRT web server to update the page.
d) Packet diagram information for /12345678.html request
For packet 9, the HTTP request was:
GET /12345678.html HTTP/1.1
Part	Size
Ethernet header	14 bytes
IPv4 header	20 bytes
TCP header	20 bytes
HTTP request data	191 bytes
Entire captured frame	245 bytes
Addresses in packet 9:
•	Source MAC: 08:00:27:aa:bb:cc 
•	Destination MAC: 08:00:27:dd:ee:ff 
•	Source IP: 192.168.56.1 
•	Destination IP: 192.168.56.2 
•	Source TCP Port: 51515 
•	Destination TCP Port: 80 
e) Referrer value
The referrer value for packet 9 is:
Referer: http://192.168.56.2/
It is able to determine that the request for /12345678.html is from the main page. Navigational tracking of web servers, website analytics, broken link detection, and understanding the flow of users between web pages can all be accomplished with the use of referrer information.
f) Browser information learned by the server
The server was able to determine the browser/Client information from the User-Agent header:
Mozilla/5.0 Week06-Lab-Browser
This indicates to the server what type/name of browser or client software it is.
g) HTTP version and transport protocol
The capture used:
•	HTTP version: HTTP/1.1 
•	Transport protocol: TCP 
•	Server port: 80 
h) Connection setup packets and time
For the first HTTP connection:
Packet	Purpose
1	TCP SYN from client to server
2	TCP SYN-ACK from server to client
3	TCP ACK from client to server
4	First HTTP data transfer begins
Time taken to setup the connection from beginning to the data transfer:
Packet 1 time: 0.000000 s
Packet 4 time: 0.075000 s
Total time: 0.075 seconds
So, data transfer started after approximately 0.075 seconds.
i) Acknowledgements
Acknowledgement packets include packets 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14 and 15, because they contain the TCP ACK flag.
An ack is usually sent when a device receives TCP data or in the process of establishing and closing the TCP connection. It ensures that packets are received without error and helps to ensure reliable data transfer.


